1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
A dramatic wildlife shot is never worth putting an animal at risk.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Baiting can change an animal's natural behaviour.

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:12,000
It can draw predators toward people, roads, or places where animals may be harmed.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
And when several animals gather around food, competition can turn dangerous.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
The same rule applies behind the camera: don't chase an animal,

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
block its escape route, or get closer just to fill the frame.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Instead, use a longer lens, stay in a vehicle or at a safe legal distance,

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
and let the animal decide what happens next.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Record natural sound. Hold the shot.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Give the moment room to unfold.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Responsible wildlife filmmaking isn't about controlling nature.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
It's about being patient enough to witness it.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Protect the animal first.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The story comes second.
